      "id": 1,
      "text": "Before 1860, the town adopted the name Rock Mills when a cotton factory opened, marking the start of its textile industry.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/rock_mills_alabama",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Rock Mills Cotton Factory, founded in 1866, evolved into the Wehadkee Yarn Mills by 1905, operated until 2004, and now hosts flea markets.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/rock_mills_alabama",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Settled in 1834 after the 1832 Treaty of Cusseta opened Creek lands, Rock Mills grew around mills powered by Wehadkee Creek.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/rock_mills_alabama",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Rock Mills sits in Dixie Alley, a zone prone to severe thunderstorms and tornadoes, including the 1932 Deep South outbreak that hit many Alabama towns.",
